===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|caesurae}}

# {{senseid|en|pause}} A [[pause]] or [[interruption]] in a [[poem]], [[music]], [[building]], or [[other]] [[work]] of [[art]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1847|author=w:Thomas De Quincey|title=Milton Versus Southey and Landor|passage=The '''caesura''' is meant to fall not with the comma after ''difficult'', but after ''thou''; and there is a most effective and grand suspension intended. It is Satan who speaks— Satan in the wilderness; and he  marks, as he wishes to mark, the tremendous opposition of attitude between the two parties to the temptation.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1858|title=w:The Autocrat of the Breakfast-Table|author={{w|Oliver Wendell Holmes Sr.|Oliver Wendell Holmes}}|passage=Now, then, for my prologue.  I am not going to change my '''cæsuras''' and cadences for anybody; so if you do not like the heroic, or iambic trimeter  brachy-catalectic, you had better not wait to hear it {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1920|title=On The Art of Reading|author=w:Arthur Quiller-Couch|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16579/pg16579.html|passage=We feel of this, as we feel of a great passage in “Hamlet” or “Lear,” that here is verse at once capable of the highest sublimity and capable of sustaining its theme, of lifting and lowering it at will, with endless resource in the slide and pause of the '''caesura''', to carry it on and on.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|chapter=4|tlr=John Howard|by=w:Joris-Karl Huysmans|title=Against the Grain|original=w:À rebours|pages=54–55|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/againstgrain00huy/page/54/mode/2up|publisher=Lieber & Lewis|location=New York|passage=He disliked the texture of those stiff verses, in their official garb, their abject reverence for grammar, their mechanical division by imperturbable '''cæsuras''', always plugged at the end in the same way by the impact of a dactyl against a spondee.}}
# {{lb|en|Classical [[prosody]]}} Using two words to divide a [[metrical]] [[foot]].
# {{lb|en|typography}} The caesura [[mark]] '''[[‖]]''' or '''||'''.
#: {{syn|en|virgule<q:in its obsolete form>}}
# {{lb|en|rare}} A break of an era or other measure of history and time; where one era ends and another begins; [[turning point]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1870|title=Nuits Blanches|author=w:Robert Louis Stevenson|passage=Like the knocking at the door in ''Macbeth'', or the cry of the watchman in the ''Tour de Nesle'', they show that the horrible '''cæsura''' is over and the nightmares have fled away, because the day is breaking and the ordinary life of men is beginning to bestir itself among the streets.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2022|author=w:Stephen King|title={{w|Fairy Tale (novel)|''Fairy Tale''}}|chapter=8|page=133|passage=A quiet time. A '''caesura'''. Then everything happened almost at once, and none of it was good.}}

====Usage notes====
In poetry bearing caesuras, it is marked by a double vertical line ⟨[[‖]]⟩.

